“I Thought It Was Just a Cold… Until It Wasn’t”
by Kevin R. Mitchell

I’ve always been the type of person who doesn’t really take getting sick seriously. If I had a cold, I’d just deal with it and keep going. I didn’t like missing work or canceling plans, so I would just push through it and assume it would go away on its own.

A while back, I started feeling sick, just a sore throat and a little cough at first. Nothing too crazy. I still went to work, still went out with friends, and didn’t really change anything. Looking back, that was probably my biggest mistake.

After a few days, it got worse. The cough got deeper, I felt more tired than usual, and I started having trouble focusing on simple things. I remember sitting at work feeling like I had no energy at all, but I still didn’t want to admit that I needed to slow down.

Eventually, I had to take time off because my body just couldn’t keep up anymore. That’s when I realized I should’ve taken it seriously earlier instead of ignoring it.

While I was at home, I started looking online for advice and ended up finding this website. At first, I didn’t think it would really help, but I read through some of the experiences people shared and it actually changed how I thought about everything.

A lot of people were saying the same things:

Don’t ignore early symptoms
Stay home if you feel sick
Rest more than you think you need
Be careful around other people

It all sounded simple, but I realized I had been doing the opposite the whole time.

So I finally started doing things differently. I stayed home, got more sleep, and stopped trying to rush back into my normal routine. I also paid more attention to not being around others while I was still feeling sick.

Over time, I started feeling better, but what really stuck with me was how avoidable some of it could’ve been. If I had just slowed down earlier, I probably wouldn’t have gotten as sick as I did.

Now, I take things more seriously. If I feel even a little off, I pay attention to it instead of brushing it off. I also think more about how my actions affect other people, not just myself.

This experience definitely changed my mindset. It made me realize that being careful isn’t overreacting, it’s just being responsible.

If I had to say one thing to anyone reading this, it would be this:

Don’t wait until your body forces you to stop. Just slow down when you need to.
